// KNOWN LIMITATION (documented, not "fixable" without a parser architecture
// change -- see CLAUDE.md gotcha #1's expanded trap list): the parser has NO
// newline-awareness at all (tokens carry byte-offset spans only, no line
// numbers), so a fresh statement whose first token can also be a binary
// operator silently continues the PREVIOUS statement's expression instead of
// starting a new one. `-`, `(`, `[` were already documented as trap tokens;
// this file proves `||` (the empty-param closure literal opener, which is
// ALSO the boolean-or operator) joins that list, and that the failure mode
// can be a SILENT WRONG VALUE with zero diagnostic, not just a confusing
// error.
//
// Expected (if statements were newline-separated as CLAUDE.md's hard rule 1
// implies): "false" -- `c` is a plain copy of `a` (false), and the following
// `|| b` is a separate, discarded closure-literal statement.
//
// Actual: "true" -- `let c: bool = a` merges with the NEXT line's `|| b`
// into ONE statement, `let c: bool = (a || b)` = `false || true` = `true`.
// No compile error, no warning, identical on both backends (kryos run and
// kryos build --release).
//
// This is also the TRUE root cause of what CLAUDE.md used to describe as "a
// closure that is the tail value of a block cannot capture that block's
// earlier let bindings" (gotcha #11) -- that repro's E0102 is this same
// merge (self-referencing the let's own name via `||`), not a real
// capture-scoping limitation. See CLAUDE.md gotcha #11 for the corrected
// explanation and tests/known_failures/ (this file) for the silent-value
// variant of the same trap.
//
//     kryos run tests/known_failures/closure_pipe_continuation_silent_wrong.kry
//     kryos build --release tests/known_failures/closure_pipe_continuation_silent_wrong.kry -o /tmp/x && /tmp/x

fn main() {
    let a: bool = false
    let b: bool = true
    let c: bool = a
    || b
    println(to_string(c))
}
